# Build outputs
bin/
obj/
artifacts/
TestResults/
*.binlog

# IDE
.vs/
.idea/
*.user
*.suo
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/extensions.json

# OS
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store

# Secrets — never committed; use user-secrets / Key Vault
*.pfx
appsettings.*.Local.json

# Frontend (future)
node_modules/
.next/

# Docker local state
.docker-data/

# Docker environment — never commit real credentials.
#
# `.env.*` rather than the narrower `.env.local`/`.env.*.local` it replaces: an `.env.uat` or
# `.env.production` written while debugging a deployment is exactly the file that must never be
# committed, and the old pattern let both through. `.env.example` is re-included because it carries
# placeholders only, and is the one file here that documents what the others must contain.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Local configuration overlays. appsettings.json and appsettings.Development.json ARE committed —
# they hold non-secret development configuration by design — but a `.local` overlay is where a
# developer puts a value that is theirs alone.
appsettings.*.local.json

# Visual Studio per-user state: window layout, startup project, the last debug target. Machine- and
# person-specific, and never meaningful to anyone else.
*.user

# Local agent/tooling state — machine-specific.
.agents/
.claude/
skills-lock.json

# Scratch for the superpowers subagent-driven-development workflow: per-plan ledgers, task briefs,
# subagent reports and review packages. Working notes for a plan in flight, not a record of it — the
# record is the git history and the plan document. Ignored so a coordination artefact can never be
# mistaken for a deliverable and committed.
.superpowers/

# Visual Studio shell "migration report", written when a VS-based tool opens a solution containing
# a project type it cannot load. SSMS produces these because it has no SSDT and cannot open the
# database project — see the note in GymOS.Backend.slnx. Generated noise, never source.
UpgradeLog*.htm

# SSDT working files. Visual Studio writes a .dbmdl model cache beside the .sqlproj and keeps it
# locked while the project is open, and .jfm alongside it. Both are per-machine caches rebuilt from
# the .sql files, never source, and the lock makes a stray `git add` fail outright.
*.dbmdl
*.dbmdl.bak
*.jfm
